item.text:
**Exposure**
Abiy Ahmed in 2022 looks like a `no_discriminating_opportunity` case for nuclear-specific conduct. Ethiopia had no nuclear arsenal to manage, but Abiy did have meaningful executive and crisis authority over the armed forces, emergency powers, foreign policy, and peace talks. So the usable 1B evidence is mostly about inherited conflict risk, de-escalation, and whether his government moved the posture safer or more dangerous by year-end.

**Reused evidence**
None.

**New evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Government | FDRE Office of the Prime Ministers","publisher":"FDRE Office of the Prime Minister","publication_date":"undated","url":"https://pmo.gov.et/government/","claim":"The highest federal executive power rests with the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers; the Prime Minister chairs the Council and is Commander-in-Chief of the National Armed Forces, while the President is head of state.","locator":"lines 30-34","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct government site describing the office structure under Abiy Ahmed's premiership.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Useful as 2022 office baseline even though the page is a current institutional summary rather than a dated 2022 archive.","ruler_attribution":"Directly identifies Abiy Ahmed's office and the executive/security powers attached to it; this is authority context, not a nuclear-policy act.","contrary_evidence":["The page is not a 2022 decision record.","It does not show any nuclear-specific choice."],"lenses":["1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Ethiopia Constitution • Page 27","publisher":"ICT Policy Africa","publication_date":"2019-09-09","url":"https://ictpolicyafrica.org/en/document/812tqdgwifg?page=27&searchTerm=right","claim":"The Constitution vests the highest executive powers in the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers, makes them responsible to the House of Peoples' Representatives, and defines the Prime Minister as chief executive, chair of the Council, and Commander-in-Chief of the national armed forces.","locator":"lines 26-64","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Legal text with specific executive powers and parliamentary constraints, directly relevant to ruler authority.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Static legal baseline applicable throughout 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Defines Abiy Ahmed's formal authority and constraints; it does not prove any particular 2022 conduct.","contrary_evidence":["This is constitutional structure, not performance evidence.","It does not show direct nuclear or existential-risk choices."],"lenses":["1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons - action details","publisher":"UN Treaty Collection","publication_date":"1970-05-25","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?clang=_en&objid=0800000280120c21","claim":"Ethiopia's action on the NPT is recorded as ratification, with registration date 25 May 1970.","locator":"lines 10-21","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN treaty record is authoritative for ratification status and date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited baseline relevant to 2022 nuclear posture and treaty constraints.","ruler_attribution":"State-level baseline rather than Abiy-specific action; it establishes that Ethiopia was already inside the nonproliferation regime.","contrary_evidence":["Ratification alone does not prove implementation quality.","It does not attribute a 2022 ruler-level decision."],"lenses":["1B.4"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia ratifies Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty","publisher":"CTBTO","publication_date":"2006-08-08","url":"https://www.ctbto.org/resources/for-the-media/press-releases/ethiopia-ratifies-comprehensive-nuclear-test-ban-treaty","claim":"Ethiopia deposited its CTBT ratification on 8 August 2006 and hosts two IMS facilities; the CTBT bans nuclear weapons test explosions and other nuclear explosions.","locator":"lines 222-229","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CTBTO press release directly states Ethiopia's ratification and verification-role status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Useful inherited baseline for 2022; it shows treaty posture, not a 2022 crisis decision.","ruler_attribution":"State treaty posture, not Abiy personal action; it shows Ethiopia was already committed to the test-ban regime.","contrary_evidence":["This does not show command decisions or internal safety governance.","It is a treaty-status fact, not a performance evaluation."],"lenses":["1B.4"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The African Nuclear-Weapon-Free Zone Treaty (Pelindaba Treaty)","publisher":"African Union","publication_date":"2022-05-06","url":"https://au.int/sites/default/files/treaties/37288-sl-THE_AFRICAN_NUCLEAR-WEAPON-FREE_ZONE_TREATY_THE_TREATY_OF_PELINDABA_2.pdf","claim":"The Pelindaba status list records Ethiopia as a signatory on 11 April 1996, a ratifier on 18 February 2008, and a deposited party on 13 March 2008.","locator":"lines 31-32","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"AU status-list PDF is the treaty registry for African nuclear-weapon-free-zone participation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited baseline relevant to 2022 because it constrains nuclear posture in Africa.","ruler_attribution":"State-level treaty baseline, not a personal Abiy action; it shows Ethiopia was within the African nuclear-weapon-free-zone regime.","contrary_evidence":["Treaty membership does not prove perfect compliance.","It does not show a 2022 ruler-level nuclear decision."],"lenses":["1B.4"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Country List : Office of Legal Affairs","publisher":"IAEA","publication_date":"2026-03-23","url":"https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=ET","claim":"Ethiopia has a safeguards agreement in force under the NPT and an Additional Protocol entry for the same safeguards relationship.","locator":"lines 112-124","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IAEA country factsheet is the authoritative registry for safeguards agreements and protocols.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Useful as a stable legal and institutional baseline for 2022, even though the page is current.","ruler_attribution":"This is state-level safeguards status, not a personal policy decision by Abiy Ahmed.","contrary_evidence":["Safeguards status does not prove the absence of any hidden risk choices.","It is an institutional constraint, not evidence of active risk reduction by the ruler."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"State of Emergency Lifted","publisher":"Ethiopian News Agency","publication_date":"2022-02-15","url":"https://www.ena.et/web/eng/w/en_33469","claim":"Ethiopia's House of Peoples' Representatives approved lifting the state of emergency on 15 February 2022; the emergency had been declared because the TPLF and allies were seen as a serious threat to national existence and sovereignty.","locator":"lines 47-49","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official state outlet reporting a dated parliamentary decision and the government rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period and relevant to crisis de-escalation in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Shows Abiy's government moving from emergency rule toward regular law-enforcement operation; this is a ruler-linked crisis-management decision.","contrary_evidence":["The conflict remained active after the emergency was lifted.","Ending the emergency did not end the broader war or human-rights crisis."],"lenses":["1B.8","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gov't Declares Indefinite Humanitarian Truce","publisher":"Ethiopian News Agency","publication_date":"2022-03-24","url":"https://www.ena.et/web/eng/w/en_34511","claim":"The Government of Ethiopia declared an indefinite humanitarian truce effective immediately on 24 March 2022 to save lives, reduce human suffering, and facilitate aid delivery to Tigray.","locator":"lines 55-71","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official statement with a precise date, purpose, and policy content.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period and central to the year's risk-de-escalation record.","ruler_attribution":"This is a ruler-attributed government decision under Abiy Ahmed's premiership, though it is still conventional-conflict rather than nuclear policy.","contrary_evidence":["The statement conditioned success on reciprocity from the other side.","Fighting later resumed, so the truce was not self-executing or durable on its own."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.8","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Release on the Cessation of Hostilities Agreement Between the Government of the FDRE and the TPLF","publisher":"African Union Commission","publication_date":"2022-11-02","url":"https://papsrepository.africanunion.org/items/037b67e6-5671-44d8-b849-f1fb03730cdf","claim":"The AU High Representative announced the successful conclusion of the Ethiopian peace talks in Pretoria, which sought a cessation of hostilities and a path to restoring peace, security, and stability in Tigray.","locator":"abstract","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official AU mediation record of the conclusion of the talks and their purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period and the key late-2022 turning point.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects Abiy Ahmed's government entering and concluding mediated peace talks; it is a strong de-escalation signal but not nuclear command evidence.","contrary_evidence":["The document records a settlement, not implementation success.","It does not address all unresolved territorial and security questions."],"lenses":["1B.8","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Obasanjo Confirms Delivery of Emergency Humanitarian Aid, Resumption of Basic Services to Tigray Region","publisher":"Ethiopian News Agency","publication_date":"2022-12-29","url":"https://www.ena.et/web/eng/w/en_41633","claim":"Olusegun Obasanjo said emergency humanitarian assistance was being provided in Tigray and basic services had started, and he said the government was implementing the peace agreement.","locator":"lines 55-64","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official report of an AU envoy's on-the-ground confirmation at the end of 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period and useful for assessing end-of-year posture after the November agreement.","ruler_attribution":"This is indirect but strong corroboration that the Abiy government was following through on the peace deal.","contrary_evidence":["Implementation was still partial and required outside verification.","The source does not prove broader security normalization across Ethiopia."],"lenses":["1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Annual Report 2022","publisher":"UNICEF Ethiopia","publication_date":"2023-04","url":"https://www.unicef.org/ethiopia/reports/annual-report-2022","claim":"UNICEF says children and families in Ethiopia faced multiple emergencies in 2022, including the northern conflict, sporadic inter-ethnic conflict, and the worst drought in forty years, producing 29.7 million people in need of humanitarian assistance.","locator":"lines 55-57","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF is a strong humanitarian monitor and the statement is directly about 2022 country conditions.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Excellent inherited-conditions baseline for 2022 rather than ruler-specific action evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Country-wide harm baseline, not personal credit or blame; it frames the risk environment Abiy inherited and governed in.","contrary_evidence":["This is humanitarian context, not proof of ruler responsibility for all harms.","It does not isolate Abiy's personal choices."],"lenses":["1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"MORNING - International Commission of Human Rights Experts on Ethiopia Finds Reasonable Grounds to Believe...","publisher":"United Nations Office at Geneva","publication_date":"2022-09-22","url":"https://www.ungeneva.org/en/news-media/meeting-summary/2022/09/le-conseil-est-informe-quil-y-des-motifs-raisonnables-de-croire","claim":"The UN experts said there were reasonable grounds to believe the federal government and allies had committed crimes against humanity in Tigray and used starvation as a method of warfare; they also noted fighting had resumed after a five-month cessation of hostilities.","locator":"lines 102-105","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN meeting summary of the commission's findings and the government's response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period and important contrary evidence for any claim that 2022 left the posture safer.","ruler_attribution":"This is adverse ruler-context evidence because the findings concern conduct of the federal government under Abiy Ahmed's premiership.","contrary_evidence":["Ethiopia disputed the report and called the commission biased.","The source addresses conflict atrocities, not nuclear policy."],"lenses":["1B.8","1B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across these buckets:
- Constitutional and office-structure pages
- UN treaty records and IAEA/CTBTO registry pages
- AU treaty/status pages
- 2022 emergency, truce, and peace-process reporting
- Humanitarian baseline reports
- Human-rights monitoring on the Tigray war
- A few out-of-period and later implementation pages

What I kept out:
- Duplicate coverage of the same event from multiple publishers when they added no materially new claim. For example, I did not keep every March 2022 truce article or every November 2022 ceasefire recap.
- Out-of-period material from 2024-2026 on Ethiopia's later nuclear-energy ambitions, later cybersecurity strategy pages, and later PMO/social media pages.
- Broad opinion/editorial pieces and generic Africa/nuclear explainers that did not add Ethiopia-specific 2022 value.
- Blocked or low-value mirrors that repeated the same legal or news text without better attribution.
- General nonproliferation fact sheets that were useful for background but not more useful than the official treaty records already kept.

**Remaining gaps**
- There is still no direct 2022 evidence of any Abiy Ahmed nuclear, CBRN, cyber, AI, or other catastrophic-risk decision with real personal authority. That is itself informative and likely supports a null exposure conclusion.
- I did not find a ruler-specific 2022 source on command-and-control discipline, expert staffing, or institutional risk management because Ethiopia had no active nuclear command structure to inspect.
- If you want to squeeze out a little more confidence on the crisis-de-escalation side, the next best search would be archived Ethiopian-language PM statements or cabinet decisions from March-November 2022, plus any contemporaneous local reporting on implementation of the humanitarian truce and Pretoria agreement.
